          "settings": {
            "heading": "Your eye cream never reached the problem — it stopped at the door",
            "image_position": "top",
            "body": "<p>The outer layer of your skin exists for one purpose: to keep things out. It does not make an exception for the $80 jar on your nightstand — which is why a luxury eye cream and a drugstore one so often perform about the same. Most of both is still sitting on the surface when you wash your face the next morning. [CITATION A — barrier function]</p><p>Cebeam’s patches take a different route. The cones on the underside are made from the actives themselves — dried, no liquid — and they sit just inside the top layer of skin, where they dissolve over the next few hours. Same ingredients your cream promised, delivered where the cream never arrived. [CITATION B — microneedle vs topical delivery]</p>"
          }
        },
        "r2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "It fixes the reason your concealer creases — instead of adding another layer on top",
            "image_position": "top",
            "body": "<p>Concealer does not crease because the concealer is bad. It creases because of the texture underneath it. You are painting a smooth coat onto an uneven wall, and by 10am the wall wins.</p><p>Because the patches work on the skin itself rather than covering it, the surface they leave behind is smoother — makeup sits on top of it instead of sinking into it. The result is not a better cover-up. It is needing less of one. Most people notice this around week three.*</p>"
          }
        },
        "r3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "It costs less than one consultation — let alone the procedure",
            "image_position": "top",
            "body": "<p>Tear-trough filler in the US typically runs <strong>$600–$1,200 a session</strong>, and it is not permanent. Lower blepharoplasty typically runs <strong>$3,000–$6,000</strong>, and it is. Both come with real recovery, real risk, and a result you cannot undo if you dislike it.</p><p>Cebeam is <strong>[XX]</strong> — less than one consultation fee at most clinics. For the very large group of women whose under-eye problem is skin quality rather than structure, it is the thing worth trying <em>before</em> you book anything.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r4": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "It was built for the camera you now spend your whole day in front of",
            "image_position": "top",
            "body": "<p>You now spend hours a day looking at your own face in high definition, under bad overhead lighting, in a small rectangle on a screen. And the under-eye is the first thing that camera finds — not your outfit, not your work, the shadows.</p><p>The patches target exactly what that camera exaggerates: the shadow cast by uneven texture, and the crepiness that catches hard light. Smoother skin reflects light more evenly, and even light is what makes a face read as rested. Not a filter — the thing the filter was imitating.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r5": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Two nights a week. No routine, no steps, nothing to remember",
            "image_position": "top",
            "body": "<p>Most eye products fail for a boring reason: nobody uses them properly. A twelve-step routine survives about nine days, then becomes a shelf of expensive jars you feel guilty about.</p><p>The entire method is this: press one patch under each eye before bed, peel them off in the morning. Two nights a week. Nothing to layer, nothing to rinse, and no order to get wrong. A product you will actually still be using in week six beats a better product you abandoned in week two.</p>"
          }
        },
        "r6": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "It will not work on everyone — and we would rather tell you now",
            "image_position": "top",
            "body": "<p>If your under-eye looks heavy because of <strong>fluid, thin skin, lost firmness, or crepey texture</strong>, that is exactly what a delivery-based topical can improve. That covers most people who arrive here.</p><p>But if your bags are <strong>inherited fat pads</strong> — the kind visible in photographs of you at twenty — no patch, cream or serum moves those. Only a surgeon does. Most people are some mix of both, which is why two women the same age get very different results. If you are mostly the second group, save your money.</p>"
          }
        },
